1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480
 In this section we are going to look at AWS Agiantic AI capabilities.

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:11,840
 So, one of the core services from AWS on Agiantic AI is Amazon Bedrock agents.

3
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:16,120
 Now, before we deep dive into what are Amazon Bedrock agents,

4
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:20,360
 let's take a look at a formal definition on Bedrock agents.

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:26,080
 So, AWS says Amazon Bedrock agents is a fully managed service.

6
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:31,840
 So, what that means is AWS will take care of hosting monitoring, scaling, patching,

7
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:35,440
 and all the other tasks related to agents.

8
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:45,000
 So, Amazon Bedrock agents enables generative AI applications to automate multi-step tasks

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,720
 by seamlessly connecting with company systems, APIs and data sources.

10
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:57,360
 And, if you look at what we had discussed in the previous section, we had talked about how agents

11
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:05,280
 can autonomously execute multi-step tasks by connecting with external data sources,

12
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:08,640
 internal data sources, through the tools.

13
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:14,840
 So, that's what AWS is highlighting on what Amazon Bedrock agents can do.

14
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:20,040
 Now, let's go and take a look at the core components of Amazon Bedrock agents.

15
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:27,280
 So, we have the agent, and then let's start with the most important planning and reasoning

16
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,560
 ability of the agents.

17
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:37,160
 So, Amazon Bedrock agent supports by default reasoning and action style of planning along

18
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:39,560
 with chain of thought reasoning.

19
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:43,320
 So, in the previous section, we had already discussed about chain of thought reasoning

20
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:46,600
 and reason and actual style of planning.

21
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,240
 So, if you're not gone through that lecture, please go through that lecture for basics in

22
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:53,240
 the previous section.

23
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:59,160
 Now, let me go ahead and deep dive a little more into how Amazon Bedrock agents actually

24
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:03,640
 implements reasoning and action style of planning.

25
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:08,880
 So, in one of the previous sections, we had looked at this use case when we were doing

26
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:11,480
 the AI agent planning demo.

27
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:17,200
 So, we said the user is going on a 5-day vacation and he sent this request to the microcool

28
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:22,600
 AI agent and then this microcool agent has two capabilities to check the leave balance of

29
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,600
 the employee and then book a hotel.

30
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:31,440
 And then we had posed this question where we said, okay, book a hotel room for me with

31
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:37,400
 employee ID this and if the leave balance is more than three days, then book a hotel.

32
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:44,200
 Now, if you remember, there were three key actions that the AI agent had to take.

33
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:50,160
 So, check my leave balance from my HR system table, then make a determination if the

34
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:55,080
 leave balance is less or more than three days and then third, if the leave balance is

35
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:59,160
 less than three days, then tell me that the hotel room cannot be booked and it's more

36
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:02,040
 than three days, then book the hotel room.

37
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:08,840
 Okay, with this example, now let's see, how this will be implemented in Amazon bedrock agents.

38
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:13,240
 So, here we are looking at the react orchestration style.

39
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:18,400
 So, sometimes it's called as react orchestration style or it can also be called as react

40
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,400
 prompting style.

41
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:23,280
 So, those are some of the keywords that we will often hear.

42
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:27,880
 Now we are the Amazon bedrock agents, so we have something known as orchestrator.

43
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:34,280
 So, you can think of orchestrator as some internal code that AWS has written for the purpose

44
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:35,280
 of orchestration.

45
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:41,440
 So, either you can use the default orchestrator that AWS has, but it also allows you to

46
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:47,200
 write the custom orchestrator in AWS Lambda function if required.

47
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:52,040
 Now, let's see what this orchestrator does and how does it interact with the large language

48
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:54,640
 model which is driving the agent.

49
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:01,000
 Okay, so the first thing this orchestrator will do is it will send the user request.

50
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,800
 So, as we saw in the previous example, the user wanted to book a hotel room based on the

51
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:06,960
 number of leaves.

52
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:12,960
 So, what this orchestrator will do is it will send the user request and all the tools

53
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:13,960
 it has.

54
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:18,560
 So, maybe it has a tool to check the leave balance from HR system, maybe it has a tool

55
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:24,960
 to check, whether maybe it has a tool which makes a booking reservation in a hotel.

56
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:31,320
 So, it will send the user request plus tools to this LLM that is driving the agent and

57
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:37,080
 then the LLM is going to analyze and then LLM is going to respond with what is the action

58
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:39,200
 that needs to be taken next.

59
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:44,240
 So, what will happen is like in our case, the first thing it's going to do is it's going

60
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:50,080
 to make a call to the HR backend system for leave balance.

61
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:56,800
 Now, let's say it has got the leave balance which says the your leave balance is 10 days.

62
00:04:56,800 --> 00:05:04,320
 Next, this orchestrator is going to send the user request, the tools and the observation

63
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:10,320
 that it got from this step which is what is my leave balance.

64
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:16,120
 So, then it's going to say okay the leave balance of the employees is 10 days and then

65
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:21,800
 the LLM is going to provide it what is the next action it needs to take.

66
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:25,960
 And in this case, since the leave balance is more than 3 days, so it's going to tell it

67
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:33,800
 go ahead and make a call to the hotel booking API and book the hotel.

68
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:39,360
 And then the orchestrator does that and once it gets the observation from the hotel booking

69
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:46,800
 API, it's going to send this request, the tools it has and the previous and the new observation

70
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:52,800
 to the agent and then agent is going to come back with its recommendation.

71
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:56,240
 And in this case, it's going to be the final result.

72
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:02,240
 And as we had seen in the demo in the previous section that it will come back with the booking

73
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:03,240
 ID.

74
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:10,360
 So that is how the reasoning and action orchestration style works in Amazon bedrock agents.

75
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:16,240
 Now if you see the react, so it's sometimes called as orchestrator style or prompting style,

76
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:23,240
 it defines the plan and then modifies as we go but it is sequential in nature.

77
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:28,200
 So now hopefully you understood that though it is dynamic, so let's say in this section

78
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:32,400
 it had said leave days is only two, so it would have taken a different action.

79
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:35,820
 But if the leave number of leave days is more than three, then it will take a different

80
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:36,820
 action.

81
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:43,040
 So though it is dynamic in nature, but it is still sequential, okay.

82
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:47,800
 The second planning style that Amazon bedrock agent supports is custom.

83
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:53,080
 So what custom means is that you can write your own orchestration or prompting style.

84
00:06:53,080 --> 00:07:00,040
 And one of the common ones is reasoning without observation or sometimes called as re-woo.

85
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:03,400
 And why would you want to use a different style of orchestration?

86
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:09,080
 And the reason is if you see the scenario that we had discussed previously, it was sequential

87
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:10,080
 in nature.

88
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:15,880
 So first the orchestrator send the request to the LLM agent, maybe if you are using something

89
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:21,240
 like cloud or a large language model, it will take three seconds maybe at least to do

90
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:26,520
 the processing then in the next step, it will again send this and there will be more processing

91
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:28,280
 and then again it is going to send.

92
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:34,080
 So it might be five to ten seconds before the user actually gets a response in complex processing

93
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:35,400
 scenarios.

94
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:39,600
 And if it is a chatbot, then the customer might not have a great user experience.

95
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:45,120
 So in some scenarios, you might just need to send the request once to the large language

96
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:51,080
 model and then it tells you steps and then you just go ahead and feedback that response

97
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:52,520
 to the end user.

98
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:56,280
 You don't need to do back and forth with the LLM.

99
00:07:56,280 --> 00:08:01,000
 So one common example that it always provides for reasoning without observation is let's

100
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,480
 say you want to book dinner reservation.

101
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:08,080
 So the only two things you have to do is let's say you want to get a menu and you want

102
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:10,920
 to book a table for a particular date.

103
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:17,280
 So once you send the request to the agent, it's going to call the restaurant reservation,

104
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:20,000
 book the table for you and then send you the menu.

105
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,920
 So maybe it needs to send that request only once to the large language model to save on

106
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:28,800
 the response time.

107
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:29,800
 Okay.

108
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:32,160
 Now the second one is memory.

109
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:37,760
 So the bedrock agents are enabled with both long term memory and shorter memory and long

110
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:44,760
 term memory can be used to store the user conversation up to 365 days.

111
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:50,200
 So long term memory is managed by AWS and all you need to do is define whether it's true

112
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:57,520
 or false and it will store the long term conversation between the user and the agent.

113
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:03,880
 Next is the shorter memory again this is enabled by AWS by default all the interaction

114
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:09,240
 between the end user and the agent within that particular session are going to be stored

115
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:16,000
 and those user inputs are also going to be passed on to the tools that you might be using

116
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,600
 and are integrated with the agent.

117
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:20,600
 Okay.

118
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:25,200
 Next is the tools and action AWS provide three kind of tools.

119
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:29,320
 So the first one is the action group and this is critical.

120
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:35,680
 So here you can use a combination of AWS Lambda and open API schema.

121
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:40,080
 So once we build the use cases in the next few sections then you are going to be able

122
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:47,600
 to visualize how we can use these action groups to connect to external and internal data

123
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:48,720
 sources.

124
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:53,520
 And then you have the bedrock knowledge basis and we've already discussed the managed

125
00:09:53,520 --> 00:10:00,280
 rag based solution from AWS and knowledge basis can be integrated with agents which allow

126
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:06,800
 the end user to access unstructured data in form of PDFs and so on.

127
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:09,120
 Then finally you have the code interpreter.

128
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:14,160
 So a common scenario would be, so let's say you have a docs agent and it's monitoring

129
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:17,560
 few applications within your environment.

130
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:20,120
 So now let's say there is an incident.

131
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:25,040
 So what this dev app agent can do is it can analyze what's the root cause of the agent

132
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:31,860
 using the LLM then generate the code and then it can test out that code in the code

133
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:35,120
 interpreter which is a local sandbox environment.

134
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:38,400
 And if it works there then it can implement that patch.

135
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:42,720
 So that is what code interpreter can be used for.

136
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,360
 So then you have the communication.

137
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:50,200
 So we have the single agent and it also supports multi agent orchestration.

138
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:55,280
 So sometimes the application that you might be building with agents might be really complicated

139
00:10:55,280 --> 00:11:01,880
 and the agent could have large number of tools maybe tens and hundreds of tools.

140
00:11:01,880 --> 00:11:08,360
 Then it might be difficult for a single agent to route the user request accurately.

141
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:14,680
 And in that scenario we can use multiple agents and then AWS provides you with a multi

142
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:20,840
 agent orchestration framework which helps you in routing to these multiple agents and

143
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:26,300
 also helping with the coordination and collaboration between these different agents.

144
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:28,400
 And then finally with the agent guardrails.

145
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:34,440
 So Amazon bedrock service already has a capability where you can build customized guardrails

146
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:39,120
 for hallucination detection or toxicity detection and control.

147
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:46,400
 So you can build the guardrails from the bedrock service and then integrated with the

148
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:48,840
 Amazon bedrock agents.

149
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:54,120
 Okay that's pretty much it from what are the core components of Amazon bedrock agents.

150
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:55,520
 I'll see you in the next lecture, thank you.

